module std.source_annotation
import std.optional { Absent, Present }
import std.algebra { empty_map, map_insert, map_lookup, map_support_keys, list_snoc_item, list_sort_by_key, int_total_order, list_at_optional, length, collection_concat_shape, list_push }
// The language-agnostic authority for source annotations, per DESIGN 4c. It names no .dag syntax and no lexical rule: a realization decides what its annotation syntax looks like and routes it through its own annotation channel, then lands rows of THIS shape. Both the v1 seed tokenizer and the v2 modeled tokenizer import from here rather than each minting a placement enum, because two spellings of one concept is the nicknaming defect this whole lane exists to remove.

import std.coercion { unicode_scalar_unfold }
import std.types { String, SourceSpan, List, Bool, Map }
import std.occurrence_identity { OccurrenceId }

// Placement is OBSERVED at capture, not reconstructed later, because it cannot be reconstructed later. Whitespace and line structure are consumed before the parser sees anything, so once they are gone `fn a() -> Int { 1 } // text` followed by `fn b()` leaves residue identical to a leading annotation on b — two opposite meanings, one byte pattern. LeadingAfterLineIndent means only indentation preceded the annotation on its line; TrailingAfterSemanticToken means a semantic token did. Placement does not decide WHERE a comment attaches -- its gap does (below) -- but it is kept on the row, because a renderer writing the comment back must know whether it sat on its own line or after code.
type AnnotationPlacement
  = LeadingAfterLineIndent
  | TrailingAfterSemanticToken

// What a tokenizer produces before any structural subject is known — hence UNBOUND. It is the shape that crosses the lexer-to-parser seam, and it deliberately carries no subject and no identity: binding is the parser's job, and minting an identity here would draw from the semantic occurrence allocator.

// WHY THIS LIVES IN std RATHER THAN IN A TOKENIZER: the v1 seed tokenizer cannot import v2 modules, so a capture shape declared inside either realization forces the other to mint a second spelling of one concept. v2's lexing module additionally carries an annotation_class Symbol on its own capture row because that lexer is generic over many languages with many annotation syntaxes; the v1 .dag tokenizer has exactly one, so it uses this shape directly. That is a real difference in what the two realizations must distinguish, not a fork — and both converge on SourceAnnotationDebt once a subject is known, which is the row that actually persists.

// THE FIELD IS `lexeme`, NOT `text`, AND THE DISTINCTION IS LOAD-BEARING. A capture carries RAW SYNTAX exactly as it appeared, delimiter included: the lexer reports what it saw, and the language-specific attachment realization owns normalization into SourceAnnotationDebt.text. Naming it `text` while storing a `//`-prefixed lexeme would make the two indistinguishable at the type level, and the failure is not hypothetical — if one realization strips the delimiter and another does not, parse then render then parse either doubles the delimiter or silently changes annotation content, and the D-C round-trip control is what would catch it. Every realization capturing into this field stores raw; every realization reading it for durable text strips.
// TWO ADJACENCY BITS, BOTH OBSERVED AT CAPTURE, BECAUSE NEITHER IS RECOVERABLE LATER. `preceded_by_blank_line` says the previous line is whitespace only — it decides where one authored block ENDS. `preceded_by_annotation_line` says the previous line is itself a standalone annotation line — it decides that this capture CONTINUES the block that line opened, which is what makes a block's subject a fact about its first line rather than something re-derived per line. The pair is not redundant: a capture with both false sits directly under a code line, and that is the only shape eligible to open a module-header block.
type UnboundAnnotationCapture {
  lexeme: String
  origin: SourceSpan
  placement: AnnotationPlacement
  preceded_by_blank_line: Bool
  preceded_by_annotation_line: Bool
}

// Every inhabitant is MODELING DEBT — that is the type's standing law, not a field on it. A comment is prose that the executable model does not currently represent, retained honestly rather than deleted or promoted. There is deliberately no rationale variant: a permanent category with no authoring path, no distinct consumer, and no lifecycle would be speculative vocabulary, and offered early every unmodeled invariant acquires a why-prefix and declares itself permanent. A second population earns its own carrier when migration produces one and its consumer is modeled.

// A COMMENT SITS IN A GAP, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE ATTACHMENT RULE. Its scope is the innermost retained source region containing it -- a parsed occurrence, a source region the semantic tree erased (the parentheses of `(x)`, the braces of a one-statement block), or the file itself when no region contains it. Its neighbours are the scope's ordered DIRECT children on either side of it. One rule covers every position: a comment above a declaration is in the module's scope with that declaration after it; one inside a function body is in that body's scope between two statements; one before a closing brace has no `after`; one after the last item, or above the module header, is in file scope. There is no positional case and no refusal: every capture has a gap.
//
// THE NEIGHBOURS ARE DIRECT CHILDREN, NEVER DESCENDANTS. For `foo(a, b)` with a comment on its own line between the arguments, the scope is the call and before = a, after = b. Picking the nearest node at ANY depth would hand the comment to a fragment of `a`; picking by proximity alone would let prose inside f's body attach to the next declaration g. Both are wrong answers the scope-then-children order makes unwritable.
//
// NOTE WHAT IS ABSENT: no gap has an identity of its own. A scope or neighbour REFERS to an occurrence the semantic parser already allocated, or to a region by its extent; attachment mints nothing, so adding, editing or deleting a comment cannot shift any occurrence identity (DESIGN 4c).
type AnnotationScope
  = FileScope
  | OccurrenceScope { occurrence: OccurrenceId }
  | ErasedSourceRegion { extent: SourceSpan }

type AnnotationGap {
  scope: AnnotationScope
  before: AnnotationScope?
  after: AnnotationScope?
}

// What a realization offers attachment: every retained source region, with its extent and its PARENT region. The parent is stated, not inferred from extents, because only the realization knows its own containment -- an erased `(x)` scope sits between `x` and x's parent, which no extent comparison alone can establish when the two share a boundary. Regions are referred to, never minted.
type AnnotationRegion {
  scope: AnnotationScope
  extent: SourceSpan
  parent: AnnotationScope
}

// `placement` is the block's first line's: where the realization's renderer writes the block back.
type SourceAnnotationDebt {
  gap: AnnotationGap
  text: String
  origin: SourceSpan
  placement: AnnotationPlacement
}

// An ORDERED sequence, because order and multiplicity are the whole of an annotation's identity here. Two annotations with the same text in the same gap are two annotations, and the one written first is written first — a set would silently merge them and a map would need the per-row key this carrier deliberately does not have. Consecutive lines group into one block at attachment, so a block is one row whose text carries its own line breaks, not N rows a consumer must re-associate.
type SourceAnnotationGraph {
  rows: List<SourceAnnotationDebt>
}

fn source_annotation_graph_empty() -> SourceAnnotationGraph {
  SourceAnnotationGraph { rows: [] }
}

fn source_annotation_graph_rows(graph: SourceAnnotationGraph) -> List<SourceAnnotationDebt> {
  graph.rows
}

// Joins the graphs of separately parsed sources, preserving order. It exists so a consumer accumulating across a compilation never reaches into `rows` to append — the ordered-sequence law lives with the carrier, and a caller that rebuilt the record itself would be free to sort, dedupe, or reverse it and silently lose the multiplicity the order encodes.
fn source_annotation_graph_concat(
  left: SourceAnnotationGraph,
  right: SourceAnnotationGraph
) -> SourceAnnotationGraph {
  SourceAnnotationGraph { rows: concat(left.rows, right.rows) }
}

fn code_point_is_line_feed(cp: Int) -> Bool {
  cp == 10
}

fn code_point_is_indent(cp: Int) -> Bool {
  cp == 32 || cp == 9
}

// Placement is decided by the PHYSICAL LINE PREFIX — the raw text between the last line feed and the annotation — and never by which lexical category the preceding lexeme belonged to.

// The category guess is wrong, and a real grammar disproves it. Deciding `leading` by `no semantic token seen on this line yet` assumes line boundaries live in trivia; the Python model's own token lexemes include a closing-paren-plus-newline-plus-indent sequence, which ENDS on a fresh line carrying only indentation, and a newline-plus-indent-plus-if sequence, which ends on a fresh line carrying semantic text. An annotation after the first is leading and after the second is trailing, yet a category rule classifies both as trailing because in both cases the last thing consumed was a semantic token. Any grammar whose tokens may span lines breaks the same way.

// So the state is `line prefix is indent only`, advanced by the RAW TEXT of every consumed lexeme regardless of category. One fold expresses the whole rule: a line feed resets the prefix to empty (vacuously indent-only), and every character after it must be indentation for the prefix to stay so. A lexeme with no line feed therefore preserves the previous state only while it is itself indentation, and a lexeme containing one is judged entirely by what follows its LAST line feed — both cases the fold produces without a special arm. An empty lexeme preserves the state.

// A lexer that walks lexemes threads this forward; a lexer with random access to the source feeds it the raw slice it consumed between steps. Both consume THIS function, because two spellings of one placement rule would let the realizations disagree about the same source.
fn advance_line_prefix_indent_only(previous: Bool, code_points: List<Int>) -> Bool {
  fold(
    code_points,
    init: previous,
    f: (acc, cp) =>
      if code_point_is_line_feed(cp: cp) { true } else { acc && code_point_is_indent(cp: cp) }
  )
}

// The rule is stated over CODE POINTS, not over a String, because the two realizations do not share a string representation: the seed's String is a host string while the modeled compiler's is already a sequence of code points. Taking the sequence as the parameter puts the one representation-specific step — how to get code points out of whatever this realization calls a lexeme — at each call site, and keeps the rule itself single-authored. The text-shaped convenience below exists for host-string callers and is the same fold, never a second rule.
fn advance_line_prefix_indent_only_text(previous: Bool, lexeme: String) -> Bool {
  advance_line_prefix_indent_only(previous: previous, code_points: unicode_scalar_unfold(s: lexeme))
}

fn placement_from_line_prefix(indent_only: Bool) -> AnnotationPlacement {
  if indent_only { LeadingAfterLineIndent } else { TrailingAfterSemanticToken }
}

// WHAT THE PREVIOUS PHYSICAL LINE HELD, observed forward by a walk, for the two adjacency bits a
// capture carries. A walk that streams its lexemes cannot look back at the source, so it threads
// this state instead, advancing it with the raw text of every consumed lexeme -- token, trivia and
// annotation alike -- exactly as it threads the line-prefix state above. A line is BLANK while only
// indentation has been seen on it, ANNOTATION-ONLY when an annotation is its first content, and CODE
// once anything else is; a trailing annotation after code leaves the line CODE. The file's first line
// has no previous line, which reads as CODE: neither bit holds. A capture reads `previous` BEFORE its
// own lexeme is folded in: blank-preceded is `previous == BlankLine`, annotation-preceded is
// `previous == AnnotationOnlyLine`.
type AnnotationLineShape
  = BlankLine
  | AnnotationOnlyLine
  | CodeLine

type AnnotationLineState {
  previous: AnnotationLineShape
  current: AnnotationLineShape
}

fn annotation_line_state_initial() -> AnnotationLineState {
  AnnotationLineState { previous: CodeLine, current: BlankLine }
}

fn advance_annotation_line_state(state: AnnotationLineState, code_points: List<Int>, is_annotation: Bool) -> AnnotationLineState {
  fold(
    code_points,
    init: state,
    f: (acc, cp) =>
      if code_point_is_line_feed(cp: cp) {
        AnnotationLineState { previous: acc.current, current: BlankLine }
      } else if code_point_is_indent(cp: cp) {
        acc
      } else {
        match acc.current {
          BlankLine => AnnotationLineState { previous: acc.previous, current: if is_annotation { AnnotationOnlyLine } else { CodeLine } }
          AnnotationOnlyLine => acc
          CodeLine => acc
        }
      }
  )
}


// The capture after the realization has stripped its delimiter. It exists as a distinct type from UnboundAnnotationCapture precisely because the `lexeme` versus `text` distinction is load-bearing: normalization is language-specific and happens exactly once, at the boundary between the realization's capture and this shared fold, so the fold can never be handed raw syntax by accident.
type NormalizedAnnotationCapture {
  text: String
  origin: SourceSpan
  placement: AnnotationPlacement
  preceded_by_blank_line: Bool
  preceded_by_annotation_line: Bool
}

fn region_contains(extent: SourceSpan, origin: SourceSpan) -> Bool {
  extent.start <= origin.start && origin.end <= extent.end
}

// THE ROSTER INDEXED ONCE PER SOURCE: each scope's direct children, ordered by start. Built in one
// pass plus one sort per sibling list, so locating a capture is a descent from the file through the
// child containing it -- a binary search per level -- rather than a scan of every region for every
// capture. The key is the scope value itself, not a text spelling of it.
type AnnotationRegionIndex {
  children: Map<AnnotationScope, List<AnnotationRegion>>
}

fn region_extent_start(r: AnnotationRegion) -> Int {
  r.extent.start
}

fn annotation_region_index(regions: List<AnnotationRegion>) -> AnnotationRegionIndex {
  let grouped_seed: Map<AnnotationScope, List<AnnotationRegion>> = empty_map()
  let grouped = fold(regions, init: grouped_seed, f: (acc, region) =>
    let key = region.parent
    match map_lookup(m: acc, key: key) {
      Present { value: siblings } => map_insert(m: acc, key: key, value: list_snoc_item(xs: siblings, item: region))
      Absent => map_insert(m: acc, key: key, value: [region])
    })
  let children_seed: Map<AnnotationScope, List<AnnotationRegion>> = empty_map()
  AnnotationRegionIndex {
    children: fold(map_support_keys(m: grouped), init: children_seed, f: (acc, key) =>
      match map_lookup(m: grouped, key: key) {
        Present { value: siblings } => map_insert(m: acc, key: key, value: list_sort_by_key(xs: siblings, key: region_extent_start, order: int_total_order()))
        Absent => acc
      }),
  }
}

fn annotation_children(index: AnnotationRegionIndex, scope: AnnotationScope) -> List<AnnotationRegion> {
  match map_lookup(m: index.children, key: scope) {
    Present { value: siblings } => siblings
    Absent => []
  }
}

// The count of siblings starting at or before `at`: the binary search every level of the descent
// and both neighbour lookups share.
fn siblings_starting_at_or_before(siblings: List<AnnotationRegion>, at: Int, lo: Int, hi: Int) -> Int {
  if lo >= hi { lo } else {
    let mid = (lo + hi) / 2
    let item: AnnotationRegion? = list_at_optional(xs: siblings, index: mid)
    match item {
      Present { value: r } =>
        if r.extent.start <= at {
          siblings_starting_at_or_before(siblings: siblings, at: at, lo: mid + 1, hi: hi)
        } else {
          siblings_starting_at_or_before(siblings: siblings, at: at, lo: lo, hi: mid)
        }
      Absent => lo
    }
  }
}

fn region_when(keep: Bool, region: AnnotationRegion?) -> AnnotationRegion? {
  match keep {
    true => region
    false => Absent
  }
}

fn region_scope(region: AnnotationRegion?) -> AnnotationScope? {
  match region {
    Present { value: r } => Present { value: r.scope }
    Absent => Absent
  }
}

// THE GAP OF ONE CAPTURE. Descend from the file: among the current scope's children -- disjoint and
// ordered -- the only one that can contain the capture is the last starting at or before it; if it
// does, it becomes the scope and the descent continues, so the innermost containing region is the
// last one reached (an argument and the expression it wraps share an extent, and the descent goes
// through the argument to the expression). When no child contains it, that same search splits the
// scope's children around the capture: the last one starting at or before it is `before`, the next
// is `after`. `depth` bounds the descent by the roster's size.
fn annotation_gap_in(index: AnnotationRegionIndex, scope: AnnotationScope, origin: SourceSpan, depth: Int) -> AnnotationGap {
  let siblings = annotation_children(index: index, scope: scope)
  let n = siblings_starting_at_or_before(siblings: siblings, at: origin.start, lo: 0, hi: length(xs: siblings))
  let candidate: AnnotationRegion? = if n == 0 { Absent } else { list_at_optional(xs: siblings, index: n - 1) }
  let inside = match candidate {
    Present { value: c } => region_contains(extent: c.extent, origin: origin) && depth > 0
    Absent => false
  }
  let containing = region_when(keep: inside, region: candidate)
  match containing {
    Present { value: inner } => annotation_gap_in(index: index, scope: inner.scope, origin: origin, depth: depth - 1)
    Absent => AnnotationGap { scope: scope, before: region_scope(region: candidate), after: region_scope(region: list_at_optional(xs: siblings, index: n)) }
  }
}

fn annotation_gap_of(index: AnnotationRegionIndex, origin: SourceSpan, depth: Int) -> AnnotationGap {
  annotation_gap_in(index: index, scope: FileScope, origin: origin, depth: depth)
}

// `pending_adjacent` records whether the immediately previous capture opened or extended `pending`. A continuation line merges into `pending` only when this is true: captures arrive in source order, so the comment line above a continuation IS the previous capture.
type AnnotationAttachAcc {
  rows: List<SourceAnnotationDebt>
  pending: SourceAnnotationDebt?
  pending_adjacent: Bool
}

fn annotation_attach_flush(acc: AnnotationAttachAcc) -> List<SourceAnnotationDebt> {
  match acc.pending {
    Absent => acc.rows
    Present { value: row } => list_push(acc.rows, row)
  }
}

// A BLOCK IS CONSECUTIVE LINES IN ONE GAP. A capture extends the open block when it continues the line above it (annotation-preceded, not blank-preceded, and the previous capture opened or extended the block) and lands in the same gap; anything else flushes the block and opens a new one. A blank line therefore splits two blocks in one gap, which keeps the author's structure: once rendered back, two blocks stay two.
//
// Adjacency cannot be recovered here — the fold sees spans, not source, and the whitespace between two captures was consumed by the lexer — so it is OBSERVED at capture, exactly like placement. `preceded_by_blank_line` and `preceded_by_annotation_line` are those observations.
//
// THE RENDERER'S OBLIGATION, the inverse of this rule: a realization rendering a graph back MUST separate two consecutive rows in one gap by a BLANK LINE, and MUST render one row's embedded line breaks as adjacent annotation lines. A renderer that emitted two rows adjacently would produce source that re-parses as ONE block, and the round trip would certify the merge.
fn annotation_attach_step(
  acc: AnnotationAttachAcc,
  capture: NormalizedAnnotationCapture,
  index: AnnotationRegionIndex,
  depth: Int
) -> AnnotationAttachAcc {
  let gap = annotation_gap_of(index: index, origin: capture.origin, depth: depth)
  let continues = capture.preceded_by_annotation_line && !capture.preceded_by_blank_line && acc.pending_adjacent
  match acc.pending {
    Present { value: open } =>
      if continues && open.gap == gap {
        AnnotationAttachAcc {
          rows: acc.rows,
          pending: Present {
            value: SourceAnnotationDebt {
              gap: open.gap,
              text: concat(open.text, concat("\n", capture.text)),
              origin: SourceSpan { file: open.origin.file, start: open.origin.start, end: capture.origin.end },
              placement: open.placement,
            },
          },
          pending_adjacent: true,
        }
      } else {
        AnnotationAttachAcc {
          rows: list_push(acc.rows, open),
          pending: Present { value: SourceAnnotationDebt { gap: gap, text: capture.text, origin: capture.origin, placement: capture.placement } },
          pending_adjacent: true,
        }
      }
    Absent =>
      AnnotationAttachAcc {
        rows: acc.rows,
        pending: Present { value: SourceAnnotationDebt { gap: gap, text: capture.text, origin: capture.origin, placement: capture.placement } },
        pending_adjacent: true,
      }
  }
}

// The single attachment authority every realization calls. It indexes the roster once, then is a PURE FOLD over captures: it reads identities and never mints one, so inserting, editing, or deleting a comment cannot shift any occurrence identity here or in any later-parsed module — there is no allocator in scope for it to violate. It cannot refuse: every capture lies in some gap, the file's at worst.
//
// Captures must arrive in source order, which is the order the lexer produced them; the block grouping above is the only thing that depends on it.
fn attach_annotations(
  captures: List<NormalizedAnnotationCapture>,
  regions: List<AnnotationRegion>
) -> SourceAnnotationGraph {
  let index = annotation_region_index(regions: regions)
  let depth = length(xs: regions) + 1
  let acc = fold(
    captures,
    init: AnnotationAttachAcc { rows: [], pending: Absent, pending_adjacent: false },
    f: (acc, capture) => annotation_attach_step(acc: acc, capture: capture, index: index, depth: depth)
  )
  SourceAnnotationGraph { rows: annotation_attach_flush(acc: acc) }
}

// WHY AGREEMENT IS KEYED ON STRINGS AND NOT ON OccurrenceId. An occurrence identity is unique within ONE graph-scoped allocator. Two separately parsed sources have two allocators, so equal id VALUES across them establish equal allocation ordinals and nothing else — a round trip comparing them would report `same gap` for two comments that merely occupy the same position in two files of the same shape. That is a FALSE POSITIVE in the oracle, the direction that never gets noticed.
//
// So agreement is stated over caller-supplied STRUCTURAL keys for the scope and both neighbours, stable ACROSS parses: the realization derives each from the region's path from the file root (its ordinal among its parent's children and its authored name, at every level). Two parses of the same structure produce the same keys; the same ordinals over different syntax produce different names and disagree.
type KeyedAnnotationRow {
  scope_key: String
  before_key: String?
  after_key: String?
  text: String
}

type KeyedAnnotationRowWalk {
  rest: List<KeyedAnnotationRow>
  agreed: Bool
}

// WHAT AGREEMENT MEANS, and deliberately what it does not. Parse a source, render its graph, parse the result: the two graphs agree when they have the same rows in the same order, each row in the same structural gap and carrying the same normalized text.
//
// EXACT SOURCE BYTES ARE NOT THE TARGET, and cannot be: normalization strips the delimiter and at most one following space, so `//tight` and `// tight` both land as `tight`. ORIGINAL OFFSETS ARE EXCLUDED too, because rendered source has its own. ORDER AND MULTIPLICITY ARE IN, because the carrier says they are its identity.
//
// It walks both row lists together, so position is part of the comparison by construction, and as a FOLD carrying the unconsumed right-hand rows rather than a recursion, so stack depth does not grow with the prose population.
fn keyed_annotation_rows_agree(
  left: List<KeyedAnnotationRow>,
  right: List<KeyedAnnotationRow>
) -> Bool {
  let walk = fold(
    left,
    init: KeyedAnnotationRowWalk { rest: right, agreed: true },
    f: (acc, row) =>
      match first(acc.rest) {
        Absent => KeyedAnnotationRowWalk { rest: [], agreed: false }
        Present { value: other } =>
          KeyedAnnotationRowWalk {
            rest: skip(acc.rest, 1),
            agreed: acc.agreed
              && row.scope_key == other.scope_key
              && row.before_key == other.before_key
              && row.after_key == other.after_key
              && row.text == other.text,
          }
      }
  )
  walk.agreed && length(xs: walk.rest) == 0
}
